// Secrets: passwords, tokens and keys kept in the system's keyring, not
// in a program's files. Each is found by a service and an account (any
// two texts: "my-tool", "dev/api_key"), and is a text.
//
//   import secrets
//   system keep() { let kept = secret_put("my-tool", "dev/api_key", "s3cr3t") }
//   system use()  { let key = secret_get("my-tool", "dev/api_key") }
//
// On Linux the keyring is the Secret Service (GNOME Keyring, KWallet,
// KeePassXC), through libsecret, which is loaded when first asked for:
// where it is not there, or no keyring answers, there are no secrets
// (`secrets_available` says so) and nothing is linked against it. On
// Windows it is the Credential Manager (a generic credential named
// "service/account"). On macOS it is the login keychain (a generic
// password of that service and account); a program that did not keep a
// secret itself, or was built again since, may be asked about by the
// system before it reads it. On a web page there are none yet. A
// keyring that is locked may ask the user to unlock it, and the call
// waits until they have.
//
// With ENT_SECRETS=none in its environment a program has no keyring on
// any system (secrets_available is false, nothing is kept or read): for
// tests, which should not touch the user's keyring.
//
// Implemented in devices/secrets.c.
import text

// Whether there is a keyring to keep secrets in.
extern proc secrets_available() -> bool
// Keeps `value` (in place of what was kept before); true where it was
// kept.
extern proc secret_put(service: text, account: text, value: text) -> bool
// What was kept; none where nothing was (or there is no keyring).
extern proc secret_get(service: text, account: text) -> text
// Forgets what was kept; true where nothing is kept then.
extern proc secret_forget(service: text, account: text) -> bool
